Evaluating $\dfrac{dy}{dx}$ at $\blueD{\theta = \dfrac{\pi}{3}}$ gives us the slope of our tangent line.

$\begin{align}
\greenD{\left. \dfrac{dy}{dx}\right| _{\theta=\tfrac{\pi}{3}}}
&=\dfrac{5\cos\left(5\left(\blueD{\dfrac{\pi}{3}}\right)\right)\sin\left(\blueD{\dfrac{\pi}{3}}\right)+\sin\left(5\left(\blueD{\dfrac{\pi}{3}}\right)\right)\cos\left(\blueD{\dfrac{\pi}{3}}\right)}{5\cos\left(5\left(\blueD{\dfrac{\pi}{3}}\right)\right)\cos\left(\blueD{\dfrac{\pi}{3}}\right)-\sin\left(5\left(\blueD{\dfrac{\pi}{3}}\right)\right)\sin\left(\blueD{\dfrac{\pi}{3}}\right)}
\\\\&=\dfrac{5\cos\left({\dfrac{5\pi}{3}}\right)\sin\left({\dfrac{\pi}{3}}\right)+\sin\left({\dfrac{5\pi}{3}}\right)\cos\left({\dfrac{\pi}{3}}\right)}{5\cos\left({\dfrac{5\pi}{3}}\right)\cos\left({\dfrac{\pi}{3}}\right)-\sin\left({\dfrac{5\pi}{3}}\right)\sin\left({\dfrac{\pi}{3}}\right)}
\\\\
&=\dfrac{5\left(\dfrac{1}{2}\right)\left(\dfrac{\sqrt{3}}{2}\right)+\left(-\dfrac{\sqrt{3}}{2}\right)\left(\dfrac{1}{2}\right)}{5\left(\dfrac{1}{2}\right)\left(\dfrac{1}{2}\right)-\left(-\dfrac{\sqrt{3}}{2}\right)\left(\dfrac{\sqrt{3}}{2}\right)}
\\\\
&=\dfrac{5\sqrt{3}-\sqrt{3}}{5+3}
\\\\&=\greenD{\dfrac{\sqrt{3}}{2}}
\end{align}$
